<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · France and the Low Countries</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Aquitaine</h1><p class="st-meta">781 CE – 877 CE · capital Toulouse</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Carolingian sub-kingdom of south-western Francia, created by Charlemagne for his son Louis the Pious.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After crushing the last independent dukes of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/early-duchy-of-aquitaine">Early Duchy of Aquitaine</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a> made <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/duchy-of-aquitaine">Aquitaine</a> a kingdom for his three-year-old son Louis, crowned by the pope in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 781. It reached from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/loire">Loire</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrenees">Pyrenees</a> and was meant to secure the frontier against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">Al-Andalus</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Louis governed with Frankish counts and advisers, reformed monasteries with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benedict-of-aniane">Benedict of Aniane</a> and led campaigns into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, taking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a> in 801. Royal residences included <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/county-of-toulouse">Toulouse</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poitiers">Poitiers</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chasseneuil-du-poitou">Chasseneuil</a>. After he became emperor in 814 the kingdom passed to his son Pepin I.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After Pepin I died in 838, his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepin-ii-of-aquitaine">Pepin II</a> fought a long war against his uncle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-the-bald">Charles the Bald</a> of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/west-francia">West Francia</a>. Charles installed his own sons, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-the-child">Charles the Child</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-stammerer">Louis the Stammerer</a>, and when Louis succeeded to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-francia">West Francia</a> in 877 the kingdom merged into it.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-aquitaine.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Aquitaine" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Patricia Fidi · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Aquitaine.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>781 CE – 877 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Toulouse</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Louis the Pious, Pepin I, Pepin II</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Latin, Old Occitan</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>France</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Carolingian Aquitaine</dd></dl></aside></div>
